The first time Paul met Larry, was in the pool hall across town late at night or more accurately in the early morning. He was playing the ghost for forty dollars a rack. He got every break but if he broke dry or missed he lost. The only way he won was if he broke, made a ball and ran out. 

Larry wasn’t betting his own money. He had a backer. That was a lot of confidence to have in some one. 

Most gamblers preferred having a backer that way there was no risk to them. If they lost it was the backer’s money if they won they backer took a significant cut, usually four to sixty percent depending on who found the game and how strong the player was. The backer usually also covered all expenses and took those off the top. 

Paul preferred betting his own money. Kevin was the only person who had ever backed him and it seemed different because they were friends. Even that made Paul a little uncomfortable. Other players said it eliminated the pressure for them but for Paul it was more pressure.  

Paul felt responsible for them and it was harder on him to lose their money than to lose his own. 

Paul watched in awe as Larry ran rack after rack. He wasn’t on for hours like a machine. He was running the counter and would take occasional bathroom breaks but mostly he just ran racks. 

He played amazing. He got out from everywhere. He played short shape for impossible outs. He banked bails that Paul was sure were double misses or too steep. 

He got down lower on the ball than anyone Paul had ever seen. He cut ball’s thinner than Paul thought possible. His bridge hand was solid as a rock. 

Larry was up for a while but as the morning reared on, it was obvious he was getting tired. He waited too long and eventually the ghost, who never tired, caught up to him. Paul didn’t know what the tally was but Larry had been losing for a while when his backer called it quits. 

After the match, Larry worked the counter for a while to himself. He must have been catching up on work that he cut corners on while playing. 

A few minutes later he went back to the table. It was about four in the morning and closing time was one AM and Paul was the only person left in the room. Larry set up a few shots he had missed still muttering to himself. 

Suddenly Larry looked up at Paul as if he saw him for the first time. “What’s your name kid?” 

Paul stuttered, “PPaul.” 

“Well PPaul, you’ve seen my worst” shaking his head. “Now you know what not to do.” 

“My God!” Paul almost shouted, “You were amazing. That was the best I’ve ever seen anyone play!” 

“You haven’t seen much then. We lost almost a thousand.” Shaking his head again. “No, I lost almost a thousand of Zack’s money. You think that’s good?” 

Paul didn’t know what to say but Larry wasn’t looking for a response and he kept going. “And we were up almost two before I got stupid. You’ve got to know when to quit. Hell, you’ve got to know when not to play!” 

Paul still didn’t know what to say and was just staring at Larry until he started to feel self conscious and looked down. 

Larry realized what was happening and said, “Sorry kid, Paul, I didn’t mean to take it out on you. Come here. You any good, let’s play a few games.” 

“Oh, wow, yeah, sure. I’m ok, nothing like you.” 

“Here, you take the first break”’ Larry said racking the balls. 

Paul was nervous and popped the cue ball off the table. 

“Settle down kid. It’s just for fun.” Laughing. 

Larry ran out from there. “Try that break again,” he said, racking “ 

This time Paul focused and hit the rack solid the eight and the four dropped. The cue ball squatted in the middle of the table and Paul had an easy shot on the one. 

“That’s it!” Larry said watching. “With that break I would have won five thousand tonight.” 

Paul tried not to let it go to his head and rattle him. He made the one got good shape on the two with the right angle to get on the three. He saw the cluster with the six and nine and left a good angle in the five for a breakout. 

He shot the five in and got the breakout but the nine rolled up on the seven. 

Larry was watching intently. “You don’t give yourself enough credit. You play well. Not many people stay on the right side of the ball and fewer still think ahead and work trouble early. Your only mistake was not noticing which way the mine was going after the breakout. It was virtually guaranteed to look up with the breakout. You should have played short side instead. 

